2. Facts out of which the present appeal arises, in short, are, that the plaintiff, who is the Chief Engineer, Roads and Highways Department, Government of the Peoples Republic of Bangladesh, instituted Title Suit No. 99 of 1992 against the defendant M/s Concord Engineers and Construction Limited, a Company incorporated under the Companies Act, with the averments, inter alia, that the Government of Bangladesh drew up a comprehensive programme for construction of two fly-overs in the city of Dhaka, namely, one at Mohakhali and the other at rail crossing on the Airport Road on Dhaka-Mymensingh highway. Accordingly, the Government invited tenders for construction of the two fly-overs at Mohakhali and Banani and the tender of the defendant, namely, M/s Concord Engineers and Construction Ltd. for Taka 78,98,30,210.00 being the lowest was accepted by the government, and work order to this effect was also issued on 15-9-90. Ultimately, the government by letter 28-2-91 regretted inability to proceed with the project and cancelled the contract agreement dated 3-9-90 entered into between the plaintiff and the defendant. Thereafter the defendant through their letter dated 11-3-91 addressed to the plaintiff submitted a claim for Taka 36 crore on account of works done together with cost of materials, etc. The plaintiff, o receipt of the claim from the defendant, informed the higher authorities of the position and the matter was under the consideration of the government. The defendant through their letter dated 30.2.91 informed the plaintiff that a dispute had arisen regarding contract about the construction of the proposed fly-overs between the plaintiff and the defendant. Thereafter the defendant appointed Mr. Justice Sultan Hossain Khan, a former Judge of the High Court Division of the Supreme Court, as their arbitrator requesting the plaintiff to appoint one Arbitrator on their behalf within 15 days, in default, the Arbitrator appointed by the defendant would arbitrate upon the dispute ex parte. The plaintiff received a notice dated 14-6-91 from the Arbitrator to the effect that he had been appointed by the defendant as the sole arbitrator in the dispute and fixed 25-6-91 for filing the statement of claims by the parties and that if no steps were taken by the plaintiff, the matter would be heard ex parte. It is the plaintiffs case that the appointment of Mr. Justice Sultan Hossain Khan as an arbitrator/sole arbitrator unilaterally by the defendant was illegal, because there was nothing in the contract agreement empowering the defendant to appoint an arbitrator and for this reason the plaintiff did not participate in the arbitration proceedings conducted by Mr. Justice Sultan Hossain Khan. In this view of the matter, the award of the arbitrator dated 21-9-91 is not an award contemplated under the Arbitration Act. In the meantime the arbitrator filed an award under section 14 of the Arbitration Act on the basis of which Arbitration Miscellaneous Case No. 71 of 1991 was registered in the 1st Court of learned Subordinate Judge, Dhaka. On 16-10-91 the plaintiff received a notice about filing of the award. The learned Subordinate Judge under his judgment and order dated 30-11-92 made the award a Rule of the court against which the plaintiff filed an appeal being First Misc. Appeal No. 82 of 1993 and also filed Title Suit No. 99 of 1992 on 6-6-92 i.e. during the pendency of Arbitration Misc. Case No. 71 of 1991 Title Suit No. 99 of 1992 was filed by the plaintiff for a declaration that the award dated 21-9-91 filed in Arbitration Misc. Case No. 71 of 1991, being not an award as contemplated under the provisions of the Arbitration Act or the agreement between the parties, is illegal, null and void, inoperative and not binding on the plaintiff and could not be made a Rule of the court. The defendant filed an application under Order 7 rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure for rejecting the plaint on the ground that it is barred by law and consequently not maintainable and the learned Subordinate Judge under the order dated 30-11-92 allowed the application and it is against the judgment and decree rejecting the plaint that the present First Appeal has been preferred. 

 (a) that an arbitrator or umpire has misconducted himself or the proceedings; 

 Section 30 seems to be exhaustive of the grounds on which an award may be set aside. The combined effect of sections 30 and 33 is that a party to an arbitration agreement or award may question the validity of the award in the manner provided for in section 33 by filing an application. In fact, the defendant filed an application in Arbitration Misc. Case No. 71 of 1991 for setting aside the award on the ground that it had no validity or existence in the eye of law and the learned Subordinate Judge rejected the application and pronounced judgment making the award a Rule of the Court against which an appeal is pending before this court.
